WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.501
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:03.934
-It's good to be back here.
-I love you.

00:00:03.934 --> 00:00:05.467
You're allowed --
Whenever you want, please,

00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:08.033
just every night of the week,
please come in.

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:10.367
I don't think I've seen you
since I saw the play.

00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:13.133
It was just so fun
and so positive

00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.267
and just amazing what you do
on that stage.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:18.067
Congrats on all of that.
-Thank you, thank you.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:21.767
-I want to ask you something,
because they just had --

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:24.667
Robert Pattinson was at the
premiere of "The Batman"

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:28.267
in London, and everyone can't
stop saying that his suit

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:32.767
looks very familiar to what you
wore in "Stop Making Sense."

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:35.367
That's real.
And that's you there.

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:39.200
And did you see
this photo or no?

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.367
-I hadn't seen this photo.

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:43.868
He's going to need a couple
of seats for that, right?

00:00:43.868 --> 00:00:44.934
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:44.934 --> 00:00:46.367
He sat in three seats
for that suit.

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:47.601
Exactly right.

00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:50.901
When you wore that suit in
"Stop Making Sense,"

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:52.300
what were you going for?

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.767
Was it fashion or was it a --

00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:55.834
-Well, one idea I had was,
I thought it would

00:00:55.834 --> 00:00:57.767
make my head look smaller.

00:00:57.767 --> 00:00:59.100
which I thought
that was a good thing.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.868
-You're actually right. It did.
You did a great job.

00:01:00.868 --> 00:01:04.000
It does look smaller. Yeah.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.300
-I was talking with
a friend who said,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.767
"Well, you know, in the theater,

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:11.200
everything has to be
bigger than real life."

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.234
And, of course, they're
referring to, like, gestures,

00:01:13.234 --> 00:01:14.934
and you talk a little bit louder

00:01:14.934 --> 00:01:17.434
and, you know,
you're a little bit larger.

00:01:17.434 --> 00:01:21.567
And I thought, "Oh, that applies
to the costumes, too."

00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:23.200
-They go, "No, no."

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.367
They weren't talking about
that at all.

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:27.234
But, yeah, you did that,
and that made it iconic.

00:01:27.234 --> 00:01:31.000
And does it come natural to you
when you're performing onstage

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.133
and talking to the audience?

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:34.200
Because not only can you
perform and sing,

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.000
but when you're just
talking and telling stories,

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.267
you're really good at it.

00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:39.934
-Well, thank you so much.
Thank you.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:41.334
No, it did not come natural.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.400
-Good. That's good to hear.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.901
-Whew!
-Really?

00:01:44.901 --> 00:01:46.033
The previews were rough?

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:47.434
-Yeah.
[ Both laugh ]

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:48.467
-Were they really?

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:49.701
'Cause when I saw it,
it was polished,

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:51.067
and I was like,
"Oh, you're a master at this."

00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:52.300
-Thank you so much.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.100
But, no, it was --
There was a learning curve.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.901
-It was, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:56.901 --> 00:02:02.000
I mean, I had to both remember
kind of the talking points,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.434
the lines, whatever,

00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:07.434
but then react to the audience
if they do something,

00:02:07.434 --> 00:02:10.067
because in this show,
they'll do something --

00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:11.968
they'll react to
something you say or do --

00:02:11.968 --> 00:02:13.901
-Yeah, if something gets
a laugh one night,

00:02:13.901 --> 00:02:15.033
it might not get a laugh
the next night.

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:16.734
-Yeah, and so you got to
react to that.

00:02:16.734 --> 00:02:19.634
And sometimes,
I realized, if you get a laugh,

00:02:19.634 --> 00:02:22.467
you can sometimes
take that further

00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:24.767
and get another laugh --
sometimes.

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:26.300
-Sometimes.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.400
But when the first one doesn't
get a laugh, you're screwed.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.634
-You better just move on.

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:32.634
-You're like, "We're doing a
20-minute show

00:02:32.634 --> 00:02:33.634
tonight, everybody.

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:35.434
Alright, goodbye. Good night."

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:36.601
It's so good.

00:02:36.601 --> 00:02:38.000
I remember reading somewhere
that you said

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.868
you've written some of
your best songs in your car.

00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:46.467
Why is that?

00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:49.901
-It was a rental car.
Just to be clear.

00:02:49.901 --> 00:02:51.467
-Okay, yeah.

00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:53.000
Of course,
it wasn't your car, yeah.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.367
-It wasn't my car.
-No.

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:58.167
-Anyway, I couldn't figure out
how to do the lyrics

00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:01.501
for this song,
so I got in a rent-a-car

00:03:01.501 --> 00:03:04.767
and drove around Minnesota.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:06.300
And --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.133
I brought them here.
-You brought them here tonight?

00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:12.801
-Yeah, I brought
them here tonight.

00:03:12.801 --> 00:03:15.667
Hot dish. Shout-out to hot dish.
-Oh, they all showed up.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:18.434
-Absolutely. I'm so psyched.
Love that.

00:03:18.434 --> 00:03:21.534
-So, I needed something to,
like, distract my mind,

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:22.868
the driving.

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:24.534
Had to pay some
attention to driving.

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:26.634
But, obviously,
we all know you can talk

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:28.300
when you're driving
and whatever else.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.934
So, I had one of those
little tape recorders.

00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:33.400
And it was just kind of
whatever came into my mind,

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.767
I would just do it

00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:36.300
and think about the song
and do that.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.067
And I started imagining --
In this particular case,

00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:43.400
I imagined, what if all
this beautiful countryside

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.033
that I'm driving through was --
It was all overgrown.

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:48.667
All the strip malls
and all the stuff

00:03:48.667 --> 00:03:51.501
that we kind of think
might be ugly,

00:03:51.501 --> 00:03:53.801
we kind of miss it
when it's gone.

00:03:53.801 --> 00:03:56.200
So I thought, "I'm going to
write a song about that."

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.033
-And there you go.
And the rest is history.

00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:01.100
And speaking of history --
How about that segue?

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.968
-Yeah, it was good. Well done.

00:04:02.968 --> 00:04:06.200
-"A History of
the World (in Dingbats)."

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.300
Can you explain what --
can you explain --

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.367
I know, I hate -- It's, like,
a loaded question.

00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:12.701
Can you explain what
a dingbat is?

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:14.067
And please don't look at me.
Please don't look at me.

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:15.634
-Yes.
-Please just look out there.

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:18.534
-Well, if you need
an explanation, yeah.

00:04:18.534 --> 00:04:21.968
-Exactly right.
But what is a dingbat?

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:25.434
-A dingbat is,
as I understand it,

00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:30.734
is not just, you know,
a person of low intelligence,

00:04:30.734 --> 00:04:34.133
but, also --
It's also a little drawing

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:36.567
that they put in
newspapers and magazines

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:39.200
to kind of sprinkle around
the text to make --

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.234
Like, they'll have
a drawing of a flower pot

00:04:41.234 --> 00:04:44.501
or, like, a little dog
or something cute like that.

00:04:44.501 --> 00:04:48.767
And I was asked for a
news magazine

00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:52.000
that I work on that's --
They asked me,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.167
"Will you do some drawings
for us that we can use

00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:57.234
to sprinkle around?"

00:04:57.234 --> 00:04:58.501
So, I said, "Okay.

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:00.167
I'll do a whole bunch of things,
and you can just do it."

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:01.601
And I got carried away.

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:07.767
And this was kind of in the
middle of the depths of COVID

00:05:07.767 --> 00:05:10.234
and then when
we were all at home a lot.

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:11.267
-Yeah.

00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:15.033
-And I realized,
"Hey, I can draw stuff."

00:05:15.033 --> 00:05:20.534
And a lot of the
drawings, I realized, were

00:05:20.534 --> 00:05:24.767
my anxieties and humor
and everything all about

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:28.067
the COVID coming out
in these little drawings,

00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:29.834
some of them funny,
some of them --

00:05:29.834 --> 00:05:31.834
you know, like,
drawings of, like,

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:34.367
weird body shapes
and body stuff,

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:39.267
when we realize our bodies are
kind of trapping us

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:42.000
in our homes and we can't go out
because our bodies

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.634
will make us ill
or we'll catch something,

00:05:44.634 --> 00:05:46.100
all these kinds of things.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.167
-Yeah, it gets --
But I love this one here,

00:05:48.167 --> 00:05:52.434
because you say,
"We're all in this together."

00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:56.033
And then, Can you see the --
-Oh, there it is. That's good.

00:05:56.033 --> 00:05:57.534
-Like, I get it. I love it.

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:59.000
-Yeah, that's kind of
what it felt like,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.367
that we're all kind of
in it together,

00:06:01.367 --> 00:06:05.234
but trapped in a thing.

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:07.033
-I love that you're always doing
something different.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.167
You've never done
the exact same thing.

00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:11.601
You've been on our show
for another book you wrote.

00:06:11.601 --> 00:06:14.767
It was amazing.
About actual sound.

00:06:14.767 --> 00:06:17.501
And now you're doing dingbats.

00:06:17.501 --> 00:06:21.067
-Now I'm doing dingbats.
-That's what I'm talking about.

00:06:21.067 --> 00:06:22.167
I want to thank you so much.

00:06:22.167 --> 00:06:23.534
You're performing for us
later tonight.

00:06:23.534 --> 00:06:25.200
What song are
you playing for us?

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.133
-We're doing a song called
"Like Humans Do."

00:06:28.133 --> 00:06:32.834
During when the Omicron came,
like, during the holidays,

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:36.000
as it did here in this show,
we were decimated.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.234
Half the band was in quarantine,
the crew, everybody.

00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:42.367
So, at one point,
some of the band said to me,

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:45.167
"David, we can throw
a show together.

00:06:45.167 --> 00:06:47.000
It won't be our show.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.501
It won't be this show.

00:06:48.501 --> 00:06:51.033
But we can learn to do
other songs,

00:06:51.033 --> 00:06:54.000
songs that we can do,
even with missing members."

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.801
So, that's what we did.

00:06:55.801 --> 00:06:58.400
So, for like
a week or 10 days, we did,

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.634
what we called
our "Unchained" show.

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:02.467
So, this is a song from that.

00:07:02.467 --> 00:07:04.567
-I appreciate you.
I'm so happy that you're here.

00:07:04.567 --> 00:07:06.601
Thank you so much.
David Byrne, everybody.

00:07:06.601 --> 00:07:08.801
"David Byrne's American Utopia"

00:07:08.801 --> 00:07:11.834
runs through April 3rd
at the St. James Theatre.

00:07:11.834 --> 00:07:14.033
And then performing for us
after the break.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.667
And "A History of
the World (in Dingbats)."

00:07:15.667 --> 00:07:17.167
Come on. This is so fun.

00:07:17.167 --> 00:07:18.868
We'll be back, everybody,
with more "Tonight Show."

00:07:18.868 --> 00:07:20.133
Come on back.

